> monster kills (assuming that's what lead to the crashes), 

That is not what leads to the crashes, as extinctionists don't seem to
run into the problem at all AFAIK.  I haven't dug into the problem to
figure out exactly what's going on, but from what I know so far, I'm
going to say it's eventual memory issues related to having 10,000+ items
on a single square (deathdrops, see).

> mean that we should prevent huge numbers of monster kills or try to
> fix whatever is making the game crash when there are huge numbers of
> monster kills? 

Well, if we assume that what is making the game crash is the ability to
put far too many items on a single square, is the problem with the case
that generates the ridiculously out-of-bounds circumstance, or the fact
that you can generally find a way to crash _any_ program if you don't
close the holes where people can do this type of thing?  And is the
appropriate fix the one that requires a small effort (and small amount
of code) to close the single case, or the one that requires a great deal
of effort to build infrastructure and new code to prop up the single case?

(I can answer this one; you close the holes where people are able to
generate ridiculously unusual quantities of stuff, and if it still
crashes, THEN you go back and shore up the logic for 'reasonable'
amounts of stuff.)

> In any case, this is only an argument that is valid for
> public servers; I doubt you care how often my PC goes down.

This is quite untrue.  Program crashes should not occur, whether they
inconvenience a single player or multiple players (though due to the
nature of public servers, any given crash will never inconvenience more
than the player who crashed).

Do I -personally- care?  Well, actually, from my perspective as a
variant creator/designer/coder, yeah, I do -- my stuff should _never_
crash if at all possible, and so pudding farming is fixed in Sporkhack.
  Took me 2 lines of code, too.  But, I also care from an
elegance-in-design perspective; applications which crash should be fixed
to at least gracefully deal with things.

>> This is the reason that a number of suggestions for "fix farming" fail;
>> what counts as a farm?  Ten kills?  One hundred?  Some people farm
>> briefly for intrinsics and then move on -- is this bad?  It certainly
>> isn't on the level of "farm for MAXINT-1 score" that ctaboir used,
>> that's for sure.
> 
> So your definition of an acceptable level of farming is...?

"No farming at all."

My fix causes puddings' maxHP to decrease when they are cloned; in
relatively short order, further clones cannot be created and a new
"parent" pudding must be generated from scratch in some fashion.

The design goal I used was: someone who carelessly attacks puddings with
the wrong weaponry should be subject to a certain amount of splitting,
because that's why puddings split in the first place -- to add
difficulty to killing them.  So, puddings should split up to a certain
point, but that point should stop far short of "infinite".

There is also a point beyond which further splits are no longer any more
of a threat to a player than the existing splits already were, and that
is where splitting should stop as no-longer-productive in terms of the
design goal; whether I've hit the mark exactly remains to be seen, but
then again, I hardly consider my fix to be immutable in stone.  If I've
missed to one side or the other, I can easily change it.

What I can say, however, is that the abuses listed in my original major
argument are no longer possible in Sporkhack, and that is good enough
for now; if puddings have become too small of a threat, then I can tweak
the numbers a little.

>> Pudding farming is, in fact, one-dimensional and reduces the game to the
>> same after the farmer is concluded; he simply uses all his wands of
>> wishing, full healing potions, scrolls of charging, etc. and ascends.
> 
> Everyone has to play the early game, but there are two ways to finish
> the game.  (snipped)

Let me restate that for you, since you went out of your way to try to
make that a very emotional argument.

One way to finish is to go through the entire game, experiencing the
challenges as laid out in the original design of the game, and dealing
more or less effectively with those challenges, depending on individual
player skill, use of resources, and yes, a certain amount of random luck.

The other way is to reduce the game to a one-dimensonal shadow of itself
and snowplow through the challenges, largely ignoring any details
involved with those challenges because they weren't designed to stand up
against a person with (for example) three spare wands of wishing. *[0]

There will always be a certain amount of the second way available,
unless you deliberately set out to design the game from the start to NOT
have infinite resources.  However, when you make the effort involved to
follow the second way significantly less than the first,... say, by
making it automatable..., the game's design is critically flawed and
requires a revisit.

> Setting aside the score issue and the public server issue for a
> moment, let us consider those who play only on their own computers. If
> you don't enjoy the latter half of the game, and there is a option to
> bypass it, or you get a kick out of being a steamroller, and there is
> an option to permit it, why is it *wrong* to do so?

Again more emotionally charged phrasing in an inappropriate place.  You
are assuming that I consider pudding farming "wrong".  I consider it
"bad design that should be fixed"; there is a significant difference.

A player should, for any given version, feel free to use everything
_within the game_ that is available using the mechanics of the game.  In
3.4.3, pudding farming is completely available given the mechanics, and
so it is certainly not 'wrong' at all to ascend by doing so.  However,
that doesn't mean that I don't think it should be fixed in a future
version, as its existence detracts from the overall quality of the game
and can actually inhibit other changes.

(On a side note, using macros to pudding farm strikes me as rather
outside the mechanics of the game; I wonder if we'd see as many farmers
if it were not possible to reduce the entire experience to copy & paste?
 However, this is a fruitless line of thinking, as it is not possible to
prevent people from copy/pasting whatever they wish into a text-based
game that has text-based input.)

